    "message": {
     "content": "### 1. The Verdict: Is this a good offer?\n\n**It is an average initial offer, but it is not compelling enough to leave Meta as a happy IC6.**\n\nBecause you were headhunted and are satisfied in your current role, this offer does not provide enough premium to justify the risk and friction of switching companies. \n\n* **Base Salary ($235k):** This is actually a **$5,000 pay cut** from your current $240k base, and sits near the midpoint of Google\u2019s posted band ($183k\u2013$271k).\n* **Sign-On Bonus ($0):** Google frequently uses sign-on bonuses to bridge unvested equity and performance bonus timing when poaching from Meta. A $0 sign-on is standard for an *opening* offer, but unacceptable for a lateral hire at L6.\n* **Equity ($1.08M / 4 years):** While $270k/year on paper is solid, Google L6 PM equity grants often reach **$1.2M to $1.4M+** for strong candidates.\n* **The Recruiter\u2019s \"Refresher\" Pitch:** Be cautious with how the recruiter framed refreshes. While Google\u2019s stacked refreshes are valuable, **Meta also gives annual stackable refreshes.** Comparing Google\u2019s multi-year projected refreshes to your static Year 1 Meta comp is an apples-to-oranges comparison.\n\n---\n\n### 2. An Important Nuance: Google's Front-Loaded Vesting\n\nThe recruiter simplified your equity as $270,000/year ($1,080,000 \u00f7 4), but Google new-hire grants typically follow a **front-loaded vesting schedule** (commonly **33% / 33% / 22% / 12%** or **38% / 32% / 20% / 10%**):\n\n| Vesting Schedule | Year 1 Equity | Year 2 Equity | Year 3 Equity | Year 4 Equity |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **33/33/22/12 Model** | ~$356,400 | ~$356,400 | ~$237,600 | ~$129,600 |\n| **38/32/20/10 Model** | ~$410,400 | ~$345,600 | ~$216,000 | ~$108,000 |\n\n* **If front-loaded:** Your Year 1 total comp is actually **~$638k\u2013$692k** (Base + 20% bonus + Front-loaded stock), which beats Meta Year 1. However, your comp drops significantly in Years 3 and 4 unless refreshes compensate for it.\n* **Action item:** Clarify with the recruiter whether your offer letter specifies the front-loaded schedule or an even 25% per year.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Strategy: Accept, Negotiate, or Walk Away?\n\n**Negotiate.** \n\nYou hold maximum leverage:\n1. You have a stable, high-paying peer role (Meta IC6).\n2. You were sourced passively and are not in a rush to leave.\n3. Google values Meta PM talent and expects an L6 candidate to negotiate.\n\nGoogle will rarely pull an offer for making a reasonable, professional counter-proposal.\n\n---\n\n### 4. What to Ask For (Your Counter-Offer)\n\nAim for a package that puts your target annualized compensation well above Meta\u2019s baseline, while securing upfront cash to offset any unvested stock or bonus you forfeit by leaving:\n\n| Component | Google Offer | What to Counter With | Rationale |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $235,000 | **$255,000** | Removes the base pay cut; sits at the ~75th percentile of their $183k\u2013$271k band. |\n| **Equity (4-yr GSU)** | $1,080,000 | **$1,350,000** (~$337k/yr) | Pushes your equity to the top quartile of L6 bands. |\n| **Sign-On Bonus** | $0 | **$75,000 \u2013 $100,000** | Standard tool to compensate for transition risk and unvested Meta RSUs/bonus forfeit. |\n\n---\n\n### 5. Negotiation Script / Email Framework\n\nYou can adapt this framing for your conversation with the recruiter:\n\n> *\"Thank you for extending the offer. I really enjoyed meeting the team, and I\u2019m excited about the scope of the role.*\n>\n> *Because I wasn't actively looking and am in a strong position at Meta\u2014currently making $526k total comp with upcoming vesting and refresher cycles\u2014this initial package doesn\u2019t quite create the financial incentive needed for me to make the move.*\n>\n> *To feel confident walking away from my current trajectory and signing with Google, I am looking for:*\n> 1. * **Base Salary:** $255,000 (to avoid taking a step back on fixed compensation).*\n> 2. * **Equity:** $1,350,000 over 4 years.*\n> 3. * **Sign-on Bonus:** $85,000 to offset unvested equity and bonus transition timing.*\n>\n> *If we can get the package to these numbers, I\u2019d be thrilled to accept and join the team.\"*",
