    "message": {
     "content": "This is a **major win**. Your recruiter listened and went to bat for you\u2014securing a **$200,000 cash sign-on bonus** at L6 without an active third-party competing offer is near the top of what Google's compensation committee typically approves.\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Financial Breakdown: What You\u2019re Actually Making\n\nWith the $200,000 sign-on and $240,000 base, your **Year 1 compensation will be significantly higher than Meta**:\n\n| Component | Year 1 at Meta | Year 1 at Google (Front-Loaded 33%) | Year 1 at Google (Linear 25%) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $240,000 | $240,000 | $240,000 |\n| **Target Bonus** | ~$36,000 (15%) | $48,000 (20%) | $48,000 (20%) |\n| **Sign-On Bonus** | $0 | **$200,000** | **$200,000** |\n| **Equity Vesting** | ~$250,000 | ~$356,400 | ~$270,000 |\n| **Total Year 1 Comp** | **~$526,000** | **~$844,400** | **~$758,000** |\n\n* **Year 1 Delta:** You will make **$232,000 to $318,000 more** at Google in your first 12 months than you would at Meta.\n* **Years 2\u20134 Outlook:** Once the sign-on expires, your target cash will be $288k ($240k base + $48k bonus). With your initial grant plus standard stacking refreshes (~$60k/yr added each year), your steady-state comp in Years 2\u20134 will average **~$580k\u2013$650k/yr**, assuming standard performance and stable stock prices.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Is It Really Their Best Offer?\n\n**Yes.** \n* When a Google recruiter matches your current base and adds a **$200k sign-on bonus** to replace unvested equity, they have escalated this to executive comp approval. \n* Pushing for more equity or base at this stage carries diminishing returns and risks frustrating the hiring manager. Treat this as their true ceiling for an L6 lateral hire.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Critical Details to Clarify Before Deciding\n\nBefore giving your answer, ask the recruiter these logistical questions:\n\n1. **Sign-On Payment Schedule & Clawback:** \n   * Is the $200,000 paid as a single lump sum in your first 30 days, or split over Year 1 and Year 2 (e.g., $100k / $100k)?\n   * What is the clawback term? (Standard is a 1-year prorated clawback, but confirm it isn't 2 years).\n2. **Equity Vesting Schedule:**\n   * Confirm whether the $1.08M vests front-loaded (e.g., 33%/33%/22%/12%) or evenly (25%/25%/25%/25%).\n\n---\n\n### 4. Should You Take It? (Decision Matrix)\n\nBecause the money is now heavily weighted in Google's favor for Year 1, the decision comes down to **culture, career momentum, and your happiness at Meta**.\n\n#### **Take the Google Offer If:**\n* **You want to bank a massive Year 1 cash windfall:** An extra ~$250k+ in Year 1 can go straight into index funds, real estate, or savings.\n* **You want portfolio diversification:** Having both Meta IC6 and Google L6 on your resume gives you elite pedigree across two distinct Big Tech operating models.\n* **You value work-life balance and stability:** Google\u2019s culture, while matrixed, is generally less churn-and-burn than Meta\u2019s PSC (performance review) environment.\n\n#### **Stay at Meta If:**\n* **You are in line for IC7 (Director/Principal PM):** Resetting your tenure at Google means spending 12\u201318 months learning the politics, building trust, and shipping before you can build a promotion case. If you have strong momentum toward IC7 at Meta, staying pays off much faster long-term.\n* **You strongly prefer fast execution over consensus:** Meta empowers PMs to move fast, ship bottom-up, and cut red tape. Moving to Google can feel jarring if you dislike navigating multi-layered stakeholder consensus and slower launch approvals.\n\n---\n\n### Recommendation\n\n**Accept the offer.** \n\nYou entered this process passively, and through negotiation, you turned an average initial offer into a **~$800k+ Year 1 package** that completely removes the financial risk of leaving your unvested Meta RSUs on the table. Unless you are 6 months away from an IC7 promotion at Meta, the upside, cash de-risking, and career prestige make this worth taking.",
